---
title: Historical supervisor design - superseded
---
flowchart TD
  accTitle: Historical design - How a waypoint task plans, verifies and stops
  accDescr: Historical diagram retained for earlier reports and proposals. The current production replacement is the basic computer and player contract; these semantic components are not current runtime instructions. One explicit task retains its original route and budgets. An injected planner supplies a bound candidate. The supervisor offers one passive intent and waits for a correlated result. Verified outbound completion requires fresh return planning. Pause invalidates the epoch; unknown effects remain unresolved. The runtime adapter owns the actual controller lease and native receipt mapping.
  T[Explicit task and retained original evidence] --> P[Plan remaining leg]
  P -->|Exact current candidate| S[Check route, evidence and budgets]
  S -->|One passive intent| I[Wait for correlated result]
  I -->|Verified step| V[Retain progress and spent budget]
  V -->|More steps in admitted leg| S
  V -->|Outbound complete, final guard passes| P
  V -->|Return complete, final guard passes| D[Task complete]
  I -->|Uncertain side effect| U[Unknown — retain pending intent]
  S -->|Guard or budget fails| B[Stop with progress preserved]
  V -->|Post-step guard or budget fails| B
  B -->|Eligible explicit continuation, no pending effect| R[Fresh route verification]
  R -->|Remaining steps| P
  R -->|Already complete leg| V
  X[Pause or takeover] -->|Revoke epoch and cancel planner| B
  I -.->|Runtime dispatch and settlement| C[Controller lease and native receipt adapter]
